  "client_view_text": " (A write-ahead log, also known as a transaction log, is a file that contains a record of all changes made to the database, in the order they were made.)\n\n## Step 1: Understanding the write-ahead log\nA write-ahead log is a file that keeps track of all changes made to a database. It's like a journal where the database records every modification, update, or insertion before it actually writes those changes to the database itself. This log is kept in a sequence, with each entry (or transaction) dated and numbered, so it's easy to follow the order in which changes were made.\n\n## Step 2: How the write-ahead log helps recovery\nWhen a crash occurs, the database cannot necessarily be relied upon to be in a consistent state. Some changes might have been written to the database, but others might not have been. By looking at the write-ahead log, the database can determine which changes were made but not yet applied to the database. It can then redo these transactions, essentially replaying the log to get the database back to a consistent state as it was before the crash.\n\n## Step 3: Recovery process\nTo recover from a crash, the database system would first read the write-ahead log from the point where it last wrote"
}